{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.references.definition_globs`.\n\nAll selection surfaces call the shared `crate::definition_glob::validate` and `matches`. I confirmed this with a ripgrep over `src`, which found calls from the evidence input resolver (`src/inputs.rs`, `impl[InputSelector]/fn[resolve]`), the authoring tools (`fn[references]`, `fn[referencing]`), the authoring inputs (`fn[source_input]`), and the CLI commands (`status_markdown_with_github` and `info_markdown` in `src/commands.rs`). The matching rule can therefore be tested directly on the shared function, and each surface needs only to show that it applies it.\n\n**Matching rule, tested in `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]`:**\n- **Complete full name:** `foo.*.bar` does not match `prefix.foo.x.bar` or `foo.x.bar.suffix`. This checks both anchors.\n- **`*` crosses dots:** `foo.*.bar` matches both `foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.y.bar`.\n- **Other characters literal:** `foo.bar` does not match `fooxbar`, so the dot is literal. The cases `foo_bar-baz` and its `X`-substituted variants check that `_` and `-` are literal.\n- **Zero characters:** `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`, because the literal dot before `*` is still required. The zero-character case itself is exercised in `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]`. There `status requirements.*ready` selects `requirements.ready`, where `*` matches nothing, and `requirements.xready`, where it matches one character. It excludes `requirements.pending`.\n- **Empty glob invalid:** `validate(\"\")` returns an error. The same case is checked in the evidence test (`definition glob must not be empty`) and in the `references` tool test, where a `\"\"` selection is rejected.\n- **Invalid characters:** `foo[bar]` is rejected in the unit test, and `foo.[a].bar` is rejected in the evidence test with `unsupported character '['`.\n\n**Every selection uses the rule:**\n- **Evidence inputs:** `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order]` uses `foo.*.bar` and expects it to match both `foo.a.bar` and `foo.a.b.bar`, so `*` crossing dots is checked here. It checks added and removed matches and the no-match error, and it rejects invalid and empty globs.\n- **CLI filters:**\n  - `commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors` checks that `[` is rejected by both `info` and `status` with `invalid definition glob`. It also checks that `nothing.*` gives `no definitions match`, and the `status requirements.*ready` case above.\n  - `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]` runs `status rules.*` and `info rules.*` and expects both the requirement and the example. It also checks that the definition `rules.used` is not selected.\n  - `tests/commands.rs#fn[verbose_status_lists_fixme_judgments_and_globs_scope_issue_groups]` also exercises glob scoping in `status`.\n- **Authoring tools:**\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` selects with `requirements.*` and gets back both requirements and the example. It rejects `\"\"` and `bad name`, and it rejects the definition `requirements.word`.\n  - `fn[requirements_reads_select_requirements_and_examples_but_not_definitions]` covers the `habeas` tool, matching `requirements.*` to the example and requirement only.\n  - `fn[ripgrep_referencing_searches_only_referencing_files_or_nodes]` and `fn[ast_grep_referencing_restricts_the_search_as_ripgrep_does]` cover `referencing` selections that use globs.\n\n**Requirements and named examples:** the example `requirements.case` appears among the matches in the authoring tests, the evidence test, and `tests/definitions.rs`. Definitions are excluded in these same tests.\n\nI found no obligation without a test and no assertion that contradicts the requirement. The acknowledgments list is empty, and no gap needs one.",
  "references": "288667bf2256bfb2c1e3d9a893df50df3abc797b6e7682916392f4e0759318ed",
